WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.212
Part Six built scalar kernel weights and their rational error
bounds.

00:00:04.735 --> 00:00:08.371
A Mellin reading also has an oscillating complex phase.

00:00:08.894 --> 00:00:16.058
This episode adds that missing factor, then combines the signal,
weight, and phase into an actual sample constructor.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:23.041
We will begin with pairs of rational coordinates, so the complex
arithmetic has an explicit representation.

00:00:23.564 --> 00:00:28.194
Then we will build a rational rotation and refine it toward the
desired phase.

00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:32.753
Finally, we will budget the errors of the product and the finite
sum.

00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:41.773
The small numerical examples are checked arithmetic examples; the
real interpretation stays attached through the project's proved
bridge theorems.

00:00:42.295 --> 00:00:48.441
This is Part Seven of Landau Windows ACL Two: complex phases and
oscillating samples.

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:57.379
In Part Five, a complex number was a point with a horizontal real
coordinate and a vertical imaginary coordinate.

00:00:57.902 --> 00:01:01.950
A rational complex point stores those two coordinates as a pair.

00:01:02.473 --> 00:01:08.727
For example, three fifths plus four fifths times i is represented by
the pair three fifths, four fifths.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.644
The ACL2 representation uses a dotted pair.

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:18.209
The first entry is the real coordinate, and the second is the
imaginary coordinate.

00:01:18.732 --> 00:01:22.526
The complex-point predicate checks that both entries are rational.

00:01:23.048 --> 00:01:27.776
A pair of numbers is a representation, not a declaration of its
meaning.

00:01:28.298 --> 00:01:33.086
In Part Two a pair represented interval endpoints; here it
represents coordinates.

00:01:33.609 --> 00:01:35.621
We keep those two uses distinct.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:40.748
Complex addition adds corresponding coordinates.

00:01:41.271 --> 00:01:46.095
Scaling by a real rational number multiplies both coordinates by
that number.

00:01:46.618 --> 00:01:53.152
Subtraction subtracts corresponding coordinates and will let us
describe the difference between two computed points.

00:01:53.674 --> 00:01:56.935
These three definitions are short enough to see together.

00:01:57.458 --> 00:02:00.318
The arithmetic stays rational at every step.

00:02:00.841 --> 00:02:04.756
Geometrically, adding a point means adding its displacement vector.

00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:11.667
Scaling by a positive number changes the length, and scaling by a
negative number also reverses the direction.

00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:18.492
These are the operations that will add samples and multiply a phase
by a real signal times a real weight.

00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:24.924
Complex multiplication uses the rule that i squared is minus one.

00:02:25.447 --> 00:02:31.980
The real coordinate of the product is the product of the real
coordinates minus the product of the imaginary coordinates.

00:02:32.503 --> 00:02:36.891
The imaginary coordinate contains the two cross products, added
together.

00:02:37.414 --> 00:02:40.541
The source definition implements exactly that rule.

00:02:41.064 --> 00:02:45.088
Multiply three fifths plus four fifths times i by i itself.

00:02:45.611 --> 00:02:49.344
The result is minus four fifths plus three fifths times i.

00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:52.655
The vector has turned through a quarter of a circle.

00:02:53.177 --> 00:02:56.971
Starting from one, four multiplications by i return to one.

00:02:57.494 --> 00:03:05.773
Our general power function repeats complex multiplication a finite
number of times, with one plus zero times i as its base case.

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:13.136
To bound a complex point's distance from zero, use the sum of the
squares of its coordinates.

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:19.550
A point is inside the disk of nonnegative radius r exactly when that
sum is at most r squared.

00:03:20.073 --> 00:03:25.418
The ACL2 predicates use this squared comparison, without evaluating
a square root.

00:03:25.941 --> 00:03:32.353
Three fifths, four fifths lies on the unit circle, because nine
twenty-fifths plus sixteen twenty-fifths is one.

00:03:32.876 --> 00:03:38.670
The point one, one is outside the unit disk, even though each
coordinate separately is at most one.

00:03:39.192 --> 00:03:43.059
Separate coordinate bounds and a disk bound are different
guarantees.

00:03:43.582 --> 00:03:48.236
For an error disk around another point, apply the disk test to their
difference.

00:03:48.759 --> 00:03:53.814
This gives us an exact rational way to compare rational complex
approximations.

00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:58.879
The desired phase is a point on the unit circle.

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:06.492
For an angle t, it is the complex exponential of i times t, whose
coordinates are cosine t and sine t.

00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:12.955
Measure the angle in radians: on the unit circle, the angle is the
signed length of the arc from one.

00:04:13.477 --> 00:04:18.205
Positive angles move counterclockwise, and negative angles move
clockwise.

00:04:18.727 --> 00:04:20.473
Every phase has length one.

00:04:20.995 --> 00:04:27.347
The target coordinates need not be rational, so we will approximate
the direction using rational coordinates.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:35.494
Our construction will preserve the unit-circle property exactly
while controlling the angular approximation through a distance
estimate.

00:04:36.017 --> 00:04:40.211
Preserving length alone is not enough to identify the desired phase.

00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:44.709
Start with a rational parameter t.

00:04:45.232 --> 00:04:49.886
The rotation's coordinates have a common denominator, four plus t
squared.

00:04:50.409 --> 00:04:56.458
The real numerator is four minus t squared, and the imaginary
numerator is four times t.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:02.823
The denominator is always positive, so there is no zero-division
case for a rational input.

00:05:03.345 --> 00:05:07.564
Squaring the two numerators and adding gives the square of the
denominator.

00:05:08.086 --> 00:05:11.905
Therefore this rational point lies exactly on the unit circle.

00:05:12.427 --> 00:05:16.209
At parameter one, the point is three fifths, four fifths.

00:05:16.732 --> 00:05:20.283
That is not exactly the phase for an angle of one radian.

00:05:20.806 --> 00:05:26.406
The rational parameter gives a convenient nearby rotation;
refinement will correct the angle.

00:05:26.929 --> 00:05:29.753
The two full source definitions are shown here.

00:05:30.276 --> 00:05:34.082
The denominator helper returns the common positive divisor.

00:05:34.605 --> 00:05:40.241
The rotation function builds the numerator pair, then scales both
entries by its reciprocal.

00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:47.397
To approximate the phase for a larger angle, split the angle into N
equal pieces.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:54.962
Use the rational rotation at parameter t divided by N, and multiply
that rotation by itself N times.

00:05:55.485 --> 00:05:58.345
Each multiplication stays on the unit circle.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:02.783
The power evaluator is the finite loop we have already introduced.

00:06:03.306 --> 00:06:09.658
At angle one with two steps, the small-step rotation is fifteen
seventeenths, eight seventeenths.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:17.392
Squaring it gives the rational point one hundred sixty-one over two
hundred eighty-nine, two hundred forty over two hundred eighty-nine.

00:06:17.915 --> 00:06:22.606
This is an exact computed point, not a rounded sine or cosine call.

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:27.408
The remaining question is how close its direction is to the target
phase.

00:06:29.533 --> 00:06:33.315
The phase-grid function packages this repeated small rotation.

00:06:33.838 --> 00:06:38.214
Its inputs are a rational angle and a positive natural number of
steps.

00:06:38.736 --> 00:06:42.688
In the picture, the target is the gold point for one radian.

00:06:43.211 --> 00:06:46.083
One step gives the coarser rational direction.

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:52.513
Two steps move closer… and further refinement brings the direction
closer still.

00:06:53.036 --> 00:06:57.811
The plotted positions are illustrations; the proof uses a distance
bound.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:07.643
Provided the step count is at least the absolute angle, the real
interpretation bounds the distance by twice the angle squared
divided by the number of steps.

00:07:08.166 --> 00:07:12.396
This is a conservative estimate, not the observed distance in the
plot.

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:17.331
The exact unit length and the approximation error are two separate
facts.

00:07:19.467 --> 00:07:23.552
We can choose enough steps from the requested positive rational
error.

00:07:24.074 --> 00:07:33.371
The construction needs the step count to be positive, to cover the
absolute angle, and to make twice the angle squared divided by the
step count no larger than the request.

00:07:33.894 --> 00:07:40.136
The definition uses the precision fuel introduced earlier, takes the
larger requirement, and adds one.

00:07:40.659 --> 00:07:44.768
The phase-value wrapper evaluates the phase grid at that computed
count.

00:07:45.291 --> 00:07:50.067
For angle one and requested distance error one quarter, the step
count is nine.

00:07:50.589 --> 00:07:54.723
The conservative bound is then two ninths, which is less than one
quarter.

00:07:55.245 --> 00:07:58.324
A smaller error request asks for more steps.

00:07:58.847 --> 00:08:03.017
No inverse trigonometric operation is needed by this algorithm.

00:08:05.133 --> 00:08:10.297
The arithmetic theory proves that the computed phase is a rational
point in the unit disk.

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.408
It also controls how two requested precisions compare.

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:21.682
The real interpretation identifies the computed point with an
approximation to the actual complex exponential.

00:08:22.205 --> 00:08:26.677
Its distance error is at most the positive tolerance supplied to the
constructor.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.836
In fact, the rational rotations have exact unit length, and so do
their finite powers.

00:08:33.359 --> 00:08:36.462
The interpretation proves that length statement too.

00:08:36.985 --> 00:08:39.336
These facts serve different purposes.

00:08:39.859 --> 00:08:44.683
The unit bound keeps later multiplications from enlarging errors
unnecessarily.

00:08:45.206 --> 00:08:48.612
The distance estimate says that the direction is the right one.

00:08:49.135 --> 00:08:54.347
The complex target is connected by a theorem, rather than being
assumed from the picture.

00:08:56.467 --> 00:09:02.018
In a Mellin reading, the phase depends on the sample address as well
as the transform's height.

00:09:02.541 --> 00:09:03.995
Call that height H.

00:09:04.518 --> 00:09:09.427
At logarithmic address u, the required angle is minus H times u.

00:09:09.950 --> 00:09:14.095
For positive H, increasing u therefore moves the phase clockwise.

00:09:14.618 --> 00:09:19.442
This is the factor that distinguishes an oscillating reading from
the plain real reading.

00:09:19.965 --> 00:09:23.359
If the height is zero, the phase is one at every address.

00:09:23.882 --> 00:09:29.373
The scalar kernel still supplies the exponential damping and the
factorial-normalized weight.

00:09:29.895 --> 00:09:36.489
The phase changes the direction of each contribution, while the real
signal and weight determine its signed scale.

00:09:37.012 --> 00:09:41.024
Keeping the minus sign correct matters when we later interpret the
sum.

00:09:43.167 --> 00:09:48.415
The signal in this coordinate system is G_z evaluated at the
exponential of u.

00:09:48.938 --> 00:09:51.823
Usually that address is not a rational number.

00:09:52.345 --> 00:09:58.588
We therefore cannot simply insert a rounded address into the signal
evaluator and reuse the same tolerance.

00:09:59.111 --> 00:10:06.214
The project computes a rational address close to the exponential
address, and pays for transport with a variation bound.

00:10:06.736 --> 00:10:13.439
The first source card chooses a coarse address cap, a transport
factor, and a small address allowance.

00:10:13.962 --> 00:10:18.168
The next card constructs the rational point near the exponential
address.

00:10:18.691 --> 00:10:24.109
The final card evaluates the signal there and widens its interval by
the transport margin.

00:10:24.632 --> 00:10:28.317
The zero ceiling coefficient selects G_z itself.

00:10:28.839 --> 00:10:33.348
This is a composition of earlier ingredients, with the address error
included.

00:10:33.871 --> 00:10:41.035
The existing real interpretation proves that the resulting interval
contains the signal at the intended exponential address.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:46.258
The sample is a product of three approximations.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:53.568
Before choosing their fine precisions, we need magnitude caps that
control how multiplication can enlarge an error.

00:10:54.091 --> 00:10:56.782
Call the signal cap M and the weight cap K.

00:10:57.305 --> 00:11:02.020
The code calls the kernel order index; this cap K is a different
quantity.

00:11:02.542 --> 00:11:09.367
The signal cap comes from a coarse signal interval with tolerance
one, using the endpoint magnitudes and some slack.

00:11:09.889 --> 00:11:14.156
The weight cap comes from a coarse kernel upper endpoint with
additional slack.

00:11:14.679 --> 00:11:18.364
Both are computed before the final precision request is used.

00:11:18.886 --> 00:11:25.529
That order prevents a circular definition where the precision
depends on a cap that itself depends on the same precision.

00:11:26.052 --> 00:11:29.955
These are conservative caps; they do not need to be close estimates.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:36.148
Now compare an intended sample with its computed approximation.

00:11:36.671 --> 00:11:41.217
One difference comes from the signal, one from the weight, and one
from the phase.

00:11:41.739 --> 00:11:46.830
Add and subtract intermediate products so that only one factor
changes at each step.

00:11:47.353 --> 00:11:52.480
The triangle inequality bounds the whole difference by the sum of
these three contributions.

00:11:53.003 --> 00:11:55.827
A signal error is amplified by the weight cap.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:59.295
A weight error is amplified by the signal cap.

00:11:59.818 --> 00:12:03.515
A phase error is amplified by the product of both caps.

00:12:04.038 --> 00:12:08.111
The phase's unit bound keeps it from adding another magnitude
factor.

00:12:08.633 --> 00:12:11.397
This explains the budget formula on screen.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:16.962
The three primitive errors cannot simply be added without their
amplification factors.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:28.155
The project uses the same pattern for each contribution: divide the
sample error request by three times one plus the relevant
amplification factor.

00:12:28.677 --> 00:12:33.502
Multiplying back by that factor consumes at most one third of the
sample budget.

00:12:34.024 --> 00:12:43.280
The signal tolerance uses the weight cap… the weight tolerance uses
the signal cap… and the phase tolerance uses their product.

00:12:43.803 --> 00:12:48.821
The signal tolerance is also capped at one to keep its coarse-cap
argument applicable.

00:12:49.344 --> 00:13:02.079
For an illustrative signal cap of two, weight cap of three, and
sample budget of one tenth, the tolerances are one one-hundred-
twentieth… one ninetieth… and one two-hundred-tenth.

00:13:02.602 --> 00:13:06.032
The amplified contributions add up to less than a tenth.

00:13:06.555 --> 00:13:11.912
These example caps are illustrative values, not a claim about a
particular address.

00:13:12.435 --> 00:13:19.489
The general arithmetic theorem proves the budget allocation for all
nonnegative rational caps and positive requests.

00:13:21.633 --> 00:13:25.318
With the tolerances chosen, the source builds each component.

00:13:25.841 --> 00:13:29.344
The signal is the lower endpoint of its fine signal interval.

00:13:29.867 --> 00:13:34.691
Its distance from the represented real value is bounded by the full
interval width.

00:13:35.214 --> 00:13:39.541
The weight is likewise the lower endpoint of its fine nonnegative
kernel interval.

00:13:40.064 --> 00:13:46.791
The phase is the rational phase constructor at angle minus the
height times the address, using its own tolerance.

00:13:47.314 --> 00:13:54.295
Lower endpoints are specific choices of rational approximations; we
are not replacing full width by half-width.

00:13:54.818 --> 00:13:57.982
The source definitions make those choices explicit.

00:13:58.505 --> 00:14:04.286
The fine values stay within the coarse caps, by the proved interval
compatibility and width bounds.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.909
Here is the actual sample definition.

00:14:09.432 --> 00:14:17.226
Multiply the computed real signal by the computed real weight, then
scale the computed complex phase by that rational product.

00:14:17.748 --> 00:14:20.512
The result is one rational complex point.

00:14:21.035 --> 00:14:29.726
To see the sign clearly, use illustrative component values: signal
minus two, weight one half, and phase three fifths, minus four
fifths.

00:14:30.248 --> 00:14:36.188
The real multiplier is minus one, so the resulting point is minus
three fifths, four fifths.

00:14:36.711 --> 00:14:42.456
These numbers illustrate the product; they are not being asserted as
the signal at a chosen address.

00:14:42.979 --> 00:14:47.464
The actual constructor computes its own components at the allocated
precisions.

00:14:47.986 --> 00:14:54.156
A negative signal reverses the phase direction, while a nonnegative
weight only changes the size.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:01.391
The sample checker records exact rational comparisons needed by the
soundness proof.

00:15:01.914 --> 00:15:06.798
The first part checks the magnitude caps and positivity of the three
tolerances.

00:15:07.321 --> 00:15:12.036
The next part checks interval widths and the bounds on the chosen
component values.

00:15:12.559 --> 00:15:18.874
The final comparison adds the amplified error allowances and
compares them with the requested sample error.

00:15:19.397 --> 00:15:28.245
The generic phase theorem already handles every rational phase
argument; this checker is not comparing a rational point with a
trigonometric oracle.

00:15:28.768 --> 00:15:42.405
The project proves that its constructor satisfies these checks
whenever the address, damping rate, and cell weight are nonnegative
rationals, the height is rational, the order is natural, and the
requested error is a positive rational.

00:15:42.927 --> 00:15:47.279
The real bridge then turns them into the intended sample's distance
bound.

00:15:47.802 --> 00:15:51.620
A checker result and its meaning theorem do their jobs together.

00:15:53.767 --> 00:15:56.761
A finite reading needs a list of sample cells.

00:15:57.283 --> 00:16:03.186
Each cell stores a nonnegative rational logarithmic address and a
nonnegative rational cell weight.

00:16:03.709 --> 00:16:08.921
The outer object is a finite list, and each cell is a dotted pair
inside that list.

00:16:09.444 --> 00:16:12.765
The cell predicate checks this representation and domain.

00:16:13.288 --> 00:16:18.888
For illustration, take two cells at addresses zero and one half,
each with weight one half.

00:16:19.411 --> 00:16:23.871
Those are addresses and weights, not the two coordinates of a
complex point.

00:16:24.394 --> 00:16:31.085
The sample constructor uses the weight of its own cell, so its error
allowance already refers to the weighted contribution.

00:16:31.608 --> 00:16:36.383
This explicit data structure is what the finite summation function
will traverse.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:46.488
The raw sum function computes one sample from the first cell, adds
it to the sum of the remaining cells, and ends at the zero complex
pair.

00:16:47.011 --> 00:16:49.774
It is a finite recursion through the supplied list.

00:16:50.297 --> 00:16:55.036
Complex addition adds real coordinates and imaginary coordinates
separately.

00:16:55.559 --> 00:17:01.644
For a small vector illustration, add minus three fifths, four fifths
to one fifth, minus one fifth.

00:17:02.167 --> 00:17:05.112
The sum is minus two fifths, three fifths.

00:17:05.635 --> 00:17:11.732
Those are illustrative contributions, not computed values of the two
sample cells shown earlier.

00:17:12.255 --> 00:17:18.885
In the real sampler, each contribution is obtained from its address,
weight, and the common transform parameters.

00:17:19.408 --> 00:17:23.020
The definition shows that actual computation explicitly.

00:17:25.167 --> 00:17:28.318
The sum still needs a total evaluation budget.

00:17:28.841 --> 00:17:36.174
With N cells, the project gives each weighted sample a point-error
allowance equal to the sum budget divided by N plus one.

00:17:36.697 --> 00:17:39.994
The wrapper calls the raw sum with that computed allowance.

00:17:40.517 --> 00:17:44.989
For two cells and a sum budget of one tenth, each sample gets one
thirtieth.

00:17:45.512 --> 00:17:50.130
Two such distance allowances add up to one fifteenth, which is below
one tenth.

00:17:50.653 --> 00:17:53.611
This uses the complex triangle inequality.

00:17:54.133 --> 00:18:00.715
Cancellation between vectors can make the actual error smaller, but
the guarantee does not rely on cancellation.

00:18:01.238 --> 00:18:04.777
This budget pays for evaluating the chosen finite grid.

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:10.391
Mesh error and the discarded infinite tail remain separate, just as
in Part Six.

00:18:12.533 --> 00:18:17.758
The construction also preserves the ingredients of the one-sided
estimate from Part Five.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:23.698
The weights are nonnegative, the signal may have either sign, and
the phases stay in the unit disk.

00:18:24.221 --> 00:18:31.445
The magnitude of the oscillating sum plus the plain real reading can
therefore be controlled by positive signal contributions.

00:18:31.968 --> 00:18:37.083
The project proves this finite complex inequality using rational
disk arithmetic.

00:18:37.606 --> 00:18:40.018
The extra real reading is still there.

00:18:40.541 --> 00:18:45.268
We cannot drop it and claim a magnitude bound without another
justified estimate.

00:18:45.791 --> 00:18:52.870
Building the samples has made the finite quantities concrete; it has
not changed the logical form of the one-sided argument.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:19:03.509
The sample interpretation identifies each computed point with the
intended weighted complex integrand, up to its requested distance
allowance.

00:19:04.032 --> 00:19:08.674
The finite-sum interpretation adds those allowances over the
supplied cells.

00:19:09.197 --> 00:19:13.488
These bridges are proved in the repository's Lean interpretation
layer.

00:19:14.011 --> 00:19:20.859
The ACL2 development separately proves the rational computations,
checks, compatibility, and budget bounds.

00:19:21.382 --> 00:19:24.473
This still describes an evaluated finite sum.

00:19:24.995 --> 00:19:29.335
Connecting it to an improper moment also needs the mesh and tail
arguments.

00:19:29.858 --> 00:19:35.991
Connecting that moment to a pole argument needs the analytic
identification and the correct geometric data.

00:19:36.514 --> 00:19:39.932
Each bridge must stay attached to the quantity it interprets.

00:19:42.067 --> 00:19:49.315
We now have an explicit rational phase constructor, an explicit
oscillating sample, and an explicit finite sum.

00:19:49.838 --> 00:19:54.432
Their precisions are selected by formulas, and their error budgets
are theorems.

00:19:54.955 --> 00:20:00.955
The unit-circle picture is supported by rational arithmetic rather
than a built-in trigonometric call.

00:20:01.477 --> 00:20:04.980
The real meaning comes from proved interpretation results.

00:20:05.503 --> 00:20:10.024
This is another set of concrete components on the route toward Peano
Arithmetic.

00:20:10.547 --> 00:20:19.347
The full Landau reduction, its general-zero applicability, and the
final arithmetic interpretation still have their own composition
obligations.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:24.524
An evaluated sum is not automatically the final coefficient
contradiction.

00:20:25.047 --> 00:20:30.986
The point of exposing these components is that their contributions
and assumptions can be inspected separately.

00:20:33.133 --> 00:20:36.879
The chain from signal to finite complex reading is now visible.

00:20:37.402 --> 00:20:42.868
The signal interval, the kernel weight, and the phase each bring an
explicit error allowance.

00:20:43.391 --> 00:20:48.433
Magnitude caps control the product, and a separate allocation
controls the sum.

00:20:48.956 --> 00:20:54.071
The remaining analytic and geometric steps have to produce a
compatible competing estimate.

00:20:54.594 --> 00:20:58.970
Next we will turn to poles, zero covers, and the coefficient
comparison.

00:20:59.492 --> 00:21:06.583
We will keep the distinction between illustrative geometry, checked
finite certificates, and claims about actual zeros.

00:21:07.106 --> 00:21:13.203
That is how the final contradiction can eventually become a concrete
arithmetic result with its intended meaning.
